  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday Night through Friday)\nIssued at 1016 PM CST Fri Nov 20 2026\n\nSaturday night and into Sunday brings the big change to our\nweather, as the aforementioned storm system and its attendant cold\nfront move through the area by sunset Sunday evening. Showers and\nisolated thunderstorms will likely be ongoing across parts of\ncentral and northern Oklahoma early Saturday evening and should\nshift eastward through the evening. Areas north of I-40 will have\nthe best chance to see this lead activity. Additional showers will\ndevelop along the cold front to the west, which remains on track\nto begin to move into northeast Oklahoma within an hour or two of\n3 am. A lower but still nonzero chance of thunderstorms will \nexist with the frontal storms in the predawn hours Sunday,\nalthough this should further diminish with time farther east. The\ntrough axis looks to clear the area by mid to late Sunday\nafternoon, signaling the end of the potential for additional\nrainfall. Given the timing of the front, a non-diurnal temperature\ncurve should occur on Sunday, particularly across parts of\nnortheast Oklahoma. The short-term blended guidance had a\nreasonable handle on this, and the hourly temperatures Sunday are\nlargely based on these data. In addition to the cooldown, gusty\nnortherly winds will prevail behind the front Sunday. With the\npost-frontal surface high still making its way into the area\nSunday night into Monday morning, overnight lows will be\ncooler than they have been but warmer compared to what is expected\non Monday night.\n\nThe pattern for next week looks to remain active, with a couple of\nsystems that may move through the area through next weekend. What\nis certain is that temperatures next week both overnight and\nduring the daytime will remain below normal. Slightly less certain\nis whether any precipitation will result from these systems,\nalthough a dry forecast looks most plausible at this point. First\nup is a system slated for Tuesday. Uncertainty remains regarding \nits evolution but with dry air in place and not much increase \nexpected ahead of the system, precipitation appears unlikely. This\nshould bring another surge of cold air, however. Another upper \nlevel system late in the week looks only slightly better for \nprecipitation.",
